# Pantheon's plan, in John Carmack's .plan format (docs/PLAN.md): one item
# per line, under the day it was written. An unmarked line is open; the
# leader marks an item "+ " when it judges it done (or "* " the day it was
# written), "- " if dropped; bids make progress on it, whole or in parts. Marked lines are pruned once they've landed, so the file holds
# what's left: `git log -p .plan` has the rest. The order is the priority,
# a guide: the leader may take another item when it's the better next step.
# An item is a pointer: its details are in the files it names.

2026-11-10
+ shell into Soma, step 4: remaining perform case into Soma: llm (prompt assembly with replace_all and plan_open leaving the helper record). Timer, log, reveal (including hash iteration and fault injection), decided persistence, peer catch-up, status formatting, check, transaction submission, and broadcast now run in Soma over small bindings, at startup/replay as well as in the loop; pure plans have concrete Examples. Process, git and socket calls stay bindings. Floor: node behavior and speed unchanged, build/proofs/edits/e2e pass, including silent timing. The bid that moves the last of them adds the step 5 line below this one, and the leader marks this one done (docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md "Next" 4 and "Two directions").
+ shell into Soma, step 5: events' parsing into Soma: connection admission and buffered-line byte limits are in Soma too (admit_decision_of, oldest_conn, buffer_over over socket bindings), so reading lines into events is Soma's whole; select and reading bytes stay bindings. When held model requests are released is decided in Soma already (release_held), and so is what a model's output becomes (reply_events: is_limit, reset_in, the hold and backoff, and the reply's JSON: json_in_reply's slice and guards, flatten_reply). Floor: node behavior and speed unchanged, build/proofs/edits/e2e pass, pure parsers checked by Examples (docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md "Next" 5)
+ M-2: the market for n agents: a merge needs n - f approvals (f = (n-1)/3), f + 1 no votes reject it, the draw goes on at n - f reveals; market.proof's theorems restated for n; a test with 7 agents, in a new test/*.sh (a bid can't change test/e2e.sh). Needed now that the Mac mini's agents have joined, 8 agents (market.soma settle and maybe_draw, market.proof; several open tasks already: max_open_tasks, the leader's call)
M-1: the leader's pick sees each bid measured, as AlphaEvolve's evaluators measured programs: the shell applies each candidate and reports beside CI's result the OCaml lines it moves out of shell.ml (or the measure the task names), Any before and after, theorems and Examples added, a complexity measure (branches, longest function), and the time of a short e2e scenario where the deployment runs it; the pick prompt shows them as a table and the leader weighs them with its reading. Floor: the measures as a pure Soma function over a tree's files, checked by Examples; past it, recorded with the merge on chain (agent.soma pick_prompt, shell.ml, deploy/ci-command.py)
M-3: the roster follows availability (docs/DESIGN.md "Membership by heartbeat"): blocks record who precommitted; an agent absent for absent_blocks is removed by a roster transaction the quorum commits, effective at a later height, and rejoins the same way; n, f and every quorum follow the roster at each height; consensus.proof and market.proof for it; a test in a new test/*.sh
V-1: verified Soma, the other direction: code already in Soma gets precise types and proofs, a module or a property per bid: Any replaced by records and inductives where the code allows (node.soma's handle and effects, the market's obj fields), theorems for what the code promises (order_effects keeps every output once and decided ones before the snapshot). Floor: no new Any, existing proofs pass; measured by Any's count and the theorems added (docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md "Two directions")
D-6: a node that can't reach a peer says so: log the peer and the reason (unresolvable, refused, timed out) when it first fails, when the reason changes and when it connects, not every second, and status.txt lists the peers with no link and why; a test whose genesis names a peer that doesn't resolve (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-6; shell.ml's dial loop in feed and resolve, or wherever step 2 put them)
U-1: the unikernel, first step: the node's I/O as one Soma interface, a datatype of its operations like Soma's Sandbox.io (peer links, timers and a millisecond clock, files, git, model and CI requests), which shell.ml interprets; Sandbox can then interpret the same program. Floor: the binary runs over it unchanged (e2e passes, same speed), and docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md lists what Sandbox still lacks for it (docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md "Two directions, and the unikernel"; shell.ml, node.soma). Part done: every effect of the node and of the commands is a node_op (87 for the loop, the commands' I/O, the consensus snapshot as Soma's own text), interpreted by shell.ml's node_io and by node_sandbox.soma; the node's setup and the commands (init, keygen, genesis, up, status, tx, plan, edits, apply-edits, check) are node programs (cli.soma). node.soma's pure bindings are Soma's (JSON tokens, floats, transaction keys from boot events), and a node boots as a MirageOS unikernel: `pantheon image` writes its setup into a Sandbox program (node_sandbox.soma's run_node_image), which runs among binaries over the binary's peer links (Sandbox's mailbox, a select over connections), votes, agrees on every block and keeps signed.log across reboots (test/node-image.sh: two binaries and the image, the fourth agent down, so blocks need its votes; booted again three times with it still down, which found and now checks three gaps: a peer one height behind gets a node's own signed messages at that height again; a node started again stays in its round with its timers and resends what it signed at its height; a step's votes are signed before its snapshot). With its service (`pantheon service HOME HOST:PORT`, the node's runtime on the host answering nodeio's service_op operations over one TCP connection, and `pantheon image --service`), the image bids, attests after checks, leads terms and merges (test/node-image-service.sh). The binary and the image run one kernel (node_kernel.soma: the tables, peer links, signatures, files and select, over eight Sandbox-named primitives that shell.ml carries out for the binary and Sandbox for the image), and the edits machinery is Soma too (cli.soma: applying edits, a bid's tree, a checkout's changes as edits, over the shell's path and git operations). So is the host's work, in the kernel (node_host.soma: a merge's git, the files a prompt reads, the heads, tool checkouts, the model's budget, limit and held requests, the inbox's poll, and jobs: model calls, checks and CI, with CI's one-at-a-time turn), over the shell's git, path, temporary-file and process primitives, so an image keeps its own budget and limit and its service only runs primitives; shell.ml's node_open builds only the network's state. Left: the service's frames over 1 MiB
P-1: the host's diplomats run with approvals off as the user that owns every node key, the inbox tokens and the push key: nodes, mirror and inbox server as a node user with 0700 homes, each diplomat its own user sharing only its inbox. Floor: host.sh does it, checked by a new test/ on a dry run that writes what root would (no root in CI); split it if too big (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-1 "The fix's design"; scripts/host.sh, scripts/bidder-inbox.sh, deploy/host/ci-sandbox, node_host.soma hrepo_dir, docs/HOST.md, docs/SAFETY.md)
P-13: bound a node's memory against unauthenticated and Byzantine peers: authenticate before buffering, cap buffered bytes per connection and overall, votes carry no value, cap proposals per round, cap the message log by bytes (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-13; shell.ml's connection reader, consensus.soma)
D-2: model-call time limits and consensus timers use a monotonic clock, so a sleep or a clock step doesn't kill calls or fire every timer; a test that moves the wall clock under a running job (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-2; node_host.soma's jobs (j_started, IoJobs), shell.ml proc_start, and timers)
D-3: a call the node kills takes its children with it: a process group per job, and bidder.sh ends its model on SIGTERM; a test that a killed job leaves nothing behind (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-3; shell.ml proc_start and proc_kill, scripts/bidder.sh)
D-4: Grok Build's patches: GROK_PATCH_FLAGS with a lower default reasoning effort, then what its rejected bids share (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-4; scripts/bidder.sh)
D-7: a merge's votes can be read: an attestor's no carries its model's reason (attest BID no REASON) into the vote and the rejection, and status shows a pending merge's votes, reasons and the height it settles at; a test of a no with a reason through to the rejection and the status (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-7; agent.soma's attest reply, market.soma's attest rule and status_lines)
D-8: no calls on a finished task: the leader skips markq when the item's line at the head is already marked, and a node withdraws a patch request whose task closed (an inbox request leaves the inbox, so a one-at-a-time session isn't held up) and drops a patch reply for a task no longer open; a test for each (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-8; agent.soma markq and the patch reply)
bonus: an agent runs its node as a binary (the extracted OCaml program) or as a MirageOS unikernel (Require Import Sandbox), from one source, and both kinds agree on every block; the unikernel is the end form: the node all Soma but Sandbox's bindings, verifiable and isolated, with a test of a unikernel node in a network of binaries (Soma's GRADUAL.md, Sandbox; docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md)
